  "updated_at": "2026-09-22T20:50:13.046Z",
  "description": "Sense.ADC.6 turns six analog inputs into calibrated millivolt readings served over I2C, so a host reads voltages instead of managing an ADC. All sampling, filtering and timing happen on the tile. It sweeps the six channels continuously at a paced rate and publishes a filtered set at a configurable output rate, 100 Hz by default.\n\nEvery published set is coherent: all six channels come from the same instant, never a mix of old and new, and each set carries a sequence number so a host can tell a fresh reading from a repeat. Readings span 0 V to the tile's own supply and are calibrated against an internal reference, so they track the rail rather than assuming it. The supply is readable too, which doubles as the full-scale value.\n\nInputs accept source impedances up to roughly 30–50 kΩ.  Output rate (1–250 Hz), sweeps averaged per period (1–32), and filter-window length are all settable at runtime and can be stored on the tile to survive reboot.",
  "json_version": "0.6",
  "application_notes": [
    {
      "sort": 0,
      "details": "The I2C address is stored on the tile and changeable across 0x08–0x77, so several tiles can share one bus; it ships at 0x28.",
      "heading": "I2C address",
      "image_url": ""
    },
    {
      "sort": 1,
      "details": "Filtering is a boxcar average whose window is set in output periods, which doubles as mains rejection: a two-period window nulls 50 Hz at a 100 Hz output, or 60 Hz at 120 Hz, and a 100 ms window nulls both",
      "heading": "Filtering",
      "image_url": ""
    },
    {
      "sort": 2,
      "details": "Poll a little faster than the output rate, two to three times, and use the sequence number to discard repeats. The tile's clock and the host's drift apart by about a percent, so polling at exactly the output rate will occasionally read the same set twice or miss one.",
      "heading": "Polling",
      "image_url": ""
    }
  ]
}
